{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08870289-b60b-524f-b96e-8c75f1f41bc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129853 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n4\\cdot {x}_{i-1}+2\\cdot {y}_{j-1}\\le 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le 4\\cdot {x}_{i}+2\\cdot {y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n4{x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1}\\le 2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le 4{x}_{i}+2{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le 2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P).\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =2({4}^{2}-{0}^{2})(2-0)=64 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})(4-0)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 64+16\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 80\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=80.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129855 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129855 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129855 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R=[-5,-2]\\times [-6,-1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129855 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74ebb72c-3651-5f76-b6c4-40ae9170b988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129855 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}3\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =3({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =3(-2-(-5))(-1-(-6)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=45\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 45=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=45.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130039 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130039 #edb/ref 1061 22783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02381e3e-cb34-5f3b-8940-d1b55826683c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130040 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130040 #edb/ref 1061 22946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130040 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ef801e2-e7f6-542e-aab3-64391c418f88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130040 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130120 #edb/ref 1061 35550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5af32a1a-efb5-509d-a196-f411e5d46fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130121 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130121 #edb/ref 1061 35559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130121 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38972544-74f5-555d-aadd-8581d0da7887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130121 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130122 #edb/ref 1061 35700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c30b46b-78e4-5a7f-bec5-89ef0019d000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130379 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a positive, continuous function defined on a closed and bounded rectangle $R,$ and let $P = P_1\\times P_2$ be a partition of $R.$ We know that the volume $V$ bounded by the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ in the $xy$-plane satisfies\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq V \\leq U(f,P),\n$$\n\nwhere $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$ are the lower and upper Riemann sums of $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\nImagine that our initial partition $P$ consists of a few rectangles only. Let's visualize the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, as shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89ad0fd33f34cfad4f55fc2085360878460df92479f5f938ecd254420457f3f4.png)\n\nThen, we refine $P$ by adding more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ resulting in more rectangles in $P{:}$ \n\n![](images/56/5635f58e19c47d6a9cfeab49c298ce5ca7aa1d8697e1b53390bb3bc31859637c.png)\n\nRefining the partition once again, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b2/b22f267ecf28627404eb90928e44d59b2873e88adc22ffaaa0a1a3335fffa307.png)\n\nAs we continue to refine $P$ by adding more and more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ we find that the values of the lower and upper Riemann sums approach the volume $V{:}$\n\n- $L(f, P)\\to V$ from below, and\n\n- $U(f, P) \\to V$ from above.\n\nWe define the **double integral** of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130379 #edb/ref 1065 3884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130379 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b9ba8fdf-255d-53c2-832a-34ed3374abb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130379 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130417 #edb/ref 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've defined the double integral of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ (if it exists) that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Up to now, we've assumed that $f(x,y)$ is continuous and $R$ is closed and bounded. Whenever this is the case, a unique number $I$ will always exist.\n\n- For positive, continuous functions, the double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$\n\n- More generally, the double integral gives a signed volume. If $f$ is negative everywhere on $R,$ then the double integral of $f$ over $R$ is negative. If $f$ is both positive and negative on $R,$ then the double integral gives the difference between the positive and negative unsigned volumes. This is similar to the case with definite integrals for single-valued functions.\n\n- Finally, It's possible to use this definition with functions that are not continuous. If a function $f$ (not necessarily continuous) is defined over $R,$ we say that $f$ is **Riemann integrable** over $R$ if a unique number $I$ can be found that satisfies the above inequality. Many (though not all) non-continuous functions are Riemann integrable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130417 #edb/ref 1065 5824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130417 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b0889acb-60da-5e9c-9f1b-530b118b05f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130417 #edb/ref 1067 "Final Remarks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256843 #edb/ref 1080 "88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256843 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256843 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "071e0901-de13-55e8-b3d3-38e6553d7abd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256872 #edb/ref 1080 "10+{y}_{j}-3{x}_{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256872 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09f2e2ce-1f99-5cc7-b2d1-9ac1d670b688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256911 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}_{i}^{2}+2{y}_{j}^{2}+{y}_{j}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d4967b5-cf10-5833-be5f-85f6c07ea0e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256956 #edb/ref 1080 "3{x}_{i}^{2}+{y}_{j}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256956 #edb/ref 1082